<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?> 

<section xml:id="section_4_6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>2SD and theory-based confidence intervals for a single mean</title>

<subsection> 
 <title>Sleepless Nights (cont.)?</title>
<p>Recall that in a previous explanation you investigated whether, on average, students at your school get less
than 8 hours of sleep on a typical night. Even though 8 hours is often cited as the recommended amount, many
people believe that college students will average less than 8 hours. But how much less? In this exploration, you
will use the same data to estimate the average number of hours of sleep students at your school get.</p>

<activity> 
 <!--Question 1-->
 <introduction>
  <p>Let <m>\mu</m> represent the average number of hours of sleep all the students at your school got the
  previous night. The question we asked in a previous exploration was, "How much sleep (to the nearest quarter
  hour) did you get last night?" If you still have the data from that exploration, give the summary statistics
  here. If not, collect a new set of data from your class and give those summary statistics here.</p>
 </introduction>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Sample size, <m>n =</m> ?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Answers will vary with class data. one ex follows: n = 22
    </p>
  </solution>
</task>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Sample mean, <m>\bar x = </m> ?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Sample mean=6.705
    </p>
  </solution>
</task>
<task>
  <statement>
    <p>
      Sample SD, <m>s = </m>?
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      sample standard deviation =1.297
    </p>
  </solution>
</task>
</activity>

<p>Similar to what you did in a previous exploration, we could conduct a simulation to
generate a sampling distribution of the sample mean number of hours of sleep, assuming
different values for <m>\mu</m> to determine which are plausible. (In the
<url href="https://static-content.zybooks.com/applets/2021/sampling/OneSample.html?hideExtras=1">
One Mean applet</url>, you can use a slider below the population to quickly change the
value of the population mean.) Recall that such an interval is called a confidence interval.
But this would be rather tedious, so in this exploration we will consider some shortcuts to
estimating a confidence interval for <m> \mu </m></p>

<activity>
 <!--Question 2-->
<statement>
<p>Based on your sample data, what is your best guess or estimate for the value of <m>\mu</m>, that
is, the average number of hours of sleep students at your school got the previous night?</p>
</statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Answers will vary with class data.
    </p>
    <p>
      One example: 6.705, since that is the sample mean
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 3-->
<statement>
<p>Suppose you repeated the survey by asking a different group of students at your school.
Will the average for this new sample be exactly the same as the original sample average
reported in question 2? Why or why not?</p>
</statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      No, every sample will yield a different estimate of the true (population) mean hours of sleep
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

<p>A natural guess or estimate for the population average number of hours of sleep students
at your school got the previous night (<m>\mu</m>) is the observed value of the sample
average sleep time. But, we know that if we were to conduct this study again we would most
likely observe a different value for the sample average due to sampling variability—we need
to account for this in our estimate for <m>\mu</m>. To do so, we create a confidence
interval for <m>\mu</m> by using the formula 
</p>
<me>
  \text{sample mean} \pm \text{multiplier} \times (\text{SD of sample mean})
</me>
<p>To find a 95% confidence interval for <m>\mu</m> we can use the 2SD method that you saw
previously, but we need to know what to use for the SD of the sample mean.</p>

<activity>
 <!--Question 4-->
<statement>
<p>In a previous exploration you simulated the distribution of sample means from the
hypothetical population of sleep times (population 1). What did you find for the standard
deviation of the sampling distribution? How did this value compare to the standard
deviation of your sample?</p>
</statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Answers will vary with class data.
    </p>
    <p>
      Got approximately 0.32 when simulating. This value (SD of sample means) is less than SD of the data
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

<remark> 
<p>There will be less sample-to-sample variability in the sample mean than
person-to-person variability in sleep times.</p>
</remark>

<activity>
 <!--Question 5-->
<statement>
<p>Previously, we saw that <m>s/\sqrt{n}</m> could be used to approximate the standard deviation of
the sample means. We often refer to such an estimate of the standard deviation of a
statistic as the standard error. Report this value.</p>
</statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Answers will vary with class data.  One example is:
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>\frac{1297}{\sqrt{22}}</m>
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

<remark> 
<p>An approximate 2SD method for a 95% confidence interval for a population
mean <m>\mu</m> is  <m>\overline{x} \pm 2 \times s / \sqrt{n} </m> . This
method is valid when the sampling distribution follows a bell-shaped,
symmetric distribution.</p>
</remark>

<activity>
 <!--Question 6-->
<statement>
<p>Use the value of s/√n you found in #5 and the 2SD method to approximate a 95%
confidence interval for <m>\mu</m> in the context of the sleep study, and then interpret
this interval. (Hint: When interpreting the interval, be clear about what the
interval estimates. You are estimating not the individual sleep times of students
but how much sleep students at your school get on …)</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Answers will vary with class data.  One example is:
  </p>
  <p>
    6.705 +/- 2*0.28 = (6.1, 7.3) We are 95% confident that the average hours of sleep per night all
students at your college got last night was between 6.1 and 7.3 hours
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

<activity>
 <!--Question 7-->
<statement>
<p>Does the 95% confidence interval reported in #6 contain 8 (the population mean
under the original null hypothesis)? Explain how you could have known this in
advance based on your p-value from the previous sleep times exploration.</p>
</statement>
<solution>
  <p>
    Answers will vary with class data.  One example is:
  </p>
  <p>
    No, the 95% confidence interval does not contain the hypothesized 8 hour average under the
null. We already knew this because the two-sided p-value was much smaller than the
significance level of 0.05
  </p>
</solution>
</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection> 
 <title>Theory-based approach</title>
<p>Previously, you saw a theory-based approach to find the p-value when testing
hypotheses about a single population mean. This approach uses a more precise value
of the multiplier in the confidence interval formula based on the t-distribution.</p>

<activity>
 <!--Question 8-->
<statement>
<p>Go to the 
<url href="https://static-content.zybooks.com/applets/2021/tbia/TBIA.html?hideExtras=1">
Theory-Based Inference applet</url>, and select the <em>One Mean</em> option from
the <em>Scenario</em> pull-down menu. Then, you can either copy and paste your class
data or just enter the sample statistics (as recorded in #1). Press <em>Calculate</em>,
check the <em>Confidence Interval</em> box, and press <em>Calculate CI</em>.</p>
<p>Report the 95% confidence interval and compare it to the interval you found in #6.
Are they about the same? Same midpoint? Same width? Could you have anticipated this?
Explain your reasoning.</p>
</statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Answers will vary with class data.  One example is:
    </p>
    <p>
      No, the 95% confidence interval does not contain the hypothesized 8 hour average under the
null. We already knew this because the two-sided p-value was much smaller than the
significance level of 0.05
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

<p>Recall that the theory-based approach uses a theoretical probability distribution
called the t-distribution to predict the behavior of the standardized statistic
(denoted by t) and that this approach required certain validity conditions to be met.</p>

<convention> 
<p>The theory-based interval for a population mean (called a <term>one-sample
<em>t</em>-interval</term>) requires that the quantitative variable should have a
symmetric distribution or you should have at least 20 observations and the sample
distribution should not be strongly skewed.</p>
</convention>

<activity>
 <!--Question 9-->
<statement>
<p>Are the validity conditions for using the 2SD or t-distribution based method to
find a confidence interval met for your class data? Explain why or why not. </p>
</statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      Yes, the sample size was at least 20 and the observed sleep hours were not strongly skewed.
    </p>
  </solution>
</activity>

<p>In summary, the formula for a theory-based confidence interval for a population mean
(<m>\mu</m>) is given by
</p>
<me>
  \overline x \pm (\text{multiplier}) \times \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}}
</me>

</subsection>

</section>